WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.690
MINH NGUYEN: Welcome to
simplifying complex application

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.090
development using
Cloud Firestore.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.610
My name is Minh Nguyen,
and I am a product manager

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:15.500
for Cloud Firestore.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.300
We have an exciting
session today

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.700
that is composed of
two major sections.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.080
The first section covers why
Cloud Firestore, and will

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.280
include key challenges
that Firestore

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.930
solves for along with a customer
testimonial from Khan academy.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.410
The second section covers what's
new in Cloud Firestore, which

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.980
will provide a sneak peek into
exciting launches and a demo

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.930
of an upcoming feature.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:41.980
Let's get started.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:49.330
OK, face it, product
development is hard.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.330
Not only do you have to
focus on providing value

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.110
through your core
business, you also

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.330
have to build complex
applications composed

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:57.620
of several components.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.720
Applications typically
start with a database.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.390
This is followed by APIs
in front of the database

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.770
to handle client queries.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.975
Of course, you will have
to keep your data secure,

00:01:13.975 --> 00:01:17.950
so you will need an app and
Auth player to manage both users

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.330
and access to the APIs.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.995
Users expect applications to
be real time and collaborative,

00:01:24.995 --> 00:01:29.710
so you'll have to define a
synchronization protocol.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.170
And finally, since users have
internet network connectivity,

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:36.940
you'll have to add support
for offline mode as well.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.680
Often these components
are produced

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.140
by several different
vendors, and it's up to you

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.280
to link or break them together.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.190
Now, if your
application takes off,

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.890
you have to ensure that your
solution grows with demand.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:55.270
That often means charting
and scaling your solution,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.780
ensuring that your services are
set up for high availability,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.110
and finally, managing major
version upgrades in security

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.500
patches which will often incur
some level of service downtime.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.340
What if product development
could be made easier?

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.040
I'd like to introduce
you to Cloud Firestore,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.950
a purpose built document
database service

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.720
that aims to make it easy
to develop applications.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.050
Firestore is the successor
to cloud data store.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:32.090
It incorporates technology from
the Firebase Realtime Database

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.900
for rapid application to open.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.510
This service is available
both through Google Cloud

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.230
and the Firebase portfolio, and
provides seamless integrations

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.630
with both ecosystems.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.640
So what makes Firestore special?

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.830
It's built to be server-less.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.932
That means it's a
fully managed service

00:02:54.932 --> 00:02:56.640
that automatically
scales based on demand

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.210
and has no maintenance windows.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.570
It supports direct application
to database connections

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.930
and customizable security rules
to ensure that users can only

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.670
access or modify the data that
they're permitted to access.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.170
It has built-in live
data synchronization

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:19.530
and offline mode making it
effortless to build real time

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.770
applications that can withstand
a network connectivity hiccup.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.930
And finally, Firestore
has a rich array

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.940
of supported libraries
and frameworks

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.860
including those
supported by Google

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.230
and community maintained
libraries as well.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.310
One of the key
attributes of Firestore

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.380
is that it's server-less.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.190
From the external
developer's perspective,

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.760
Firestore offers a set
of APIs for document

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.910
reads, writes, and deletes.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.420
Behind the covers, the service
D couples processing and storage

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.810
layers in order to automatically
scale both dimensions

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.570
independently to meet demand.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:03.300
All data is stored across
multiple strongly consistent

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.110
replicas.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.040
In our regional
offering of Firestore,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.660
that means having an
availability SLA of 99.99%.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.802
In our multi-regional
offering of Firestore,

00:04:14.802 --> 00:04:16.260
that means having
a service that is

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.620
resilient to whole region
outage and availability

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:25.920
SLA of 99.999%.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.810
As applications
perform additional read

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.940
and write operations
on the database,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.390
the database uses automated
load and ranged-based

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:37.050
charting to scale processing
and storage with minimal impact

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.640
latency.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.700
In practice, some customers
have scaled to tens of millions

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:47.480
of requests per second,
and petabytes of storage.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.120
More interestingly, Firestore
is an elastic service

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.390
that can scale resource
utilization both up and down.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.190
As a result, customers
only pay for what they use.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.800
No capacity planning
is required.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.690
Getting started
is also completely

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.750
free with a generous,
always free tier

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:10.960
to incubate your development.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.220
Often, I get the question, how
is Firestore used in practice?

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.050
Let's go through three common
customer architectures.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.900
OK, let's say that you
want to use Firestore

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.000
with server-less
attributes, but you

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.680
prefer to architect
your solution

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.440
utilizing a more traditional
approach that utilizes

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.540
compute services to govern
access to the database.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.590
You're not, that setup
is fully supported.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.750
In this architecture,
access to Firestore

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.410
is authenticated and
authorized through Cloud IAM.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.250
Since Firestore is
transactional database,

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.070
this architecture
is often extended

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.710
to support analytical
workloads through a one click

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:56.950
integration with BigQuery
that leverages cloud

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.345
function triggers to
stream data changes.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.530
What if you're an
application development

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.380
team that wants to reduce
development time and total

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.327
cost of ownership?

00:06:10.327 --> 00:06:12.160
In this diagram, you
can see an architecture

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.010
that achieves just that.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.440
Here, all applications
communicate directly

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.600
to the database utilizing
Firestore's client libraries.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.640
Authentication is handled
through Firebase off or Google

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:28.390
Cloud Identity Platform.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:32.320
Authorization is managed through
customizable Cloud Firestore

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.970
security rules.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:38.090
Syncing is handled through
Firestore's persistent sockets

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.640
to the database.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.270
Offline mode is managed through
Firestore's on device database.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.050
Similar, the last
architecture diagram

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.790
to support analytical
workloads, we

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.650
recommend customers
set up a one click

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:53.845
integration with BigQuery.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.620
If an application needs to
present analytical results back

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.900
to the end user, customers
can execute BigQuery

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:06.010
via Cloud Functions,
and store these results

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.700
in Cloud Firestore.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.450
If live synchronization,
latest results

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.970
will automatically be
transmitted to listing

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.320
application connections.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.540
If you already have an existing
legacy application and database

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:25.340
but are facing challenges with
high availability, scaling,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.840
or simply trying to build out
features for your application,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.820
including real time
collaboration in offline mode,

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:34.440
Cloud Firestore is for you.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.030
We are frequently
seeing customers

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.820
use Cloud Firestore in front
of an existing legacy database

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.360
to take advantage of
these native capabilities.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.310
In this architecture,
Cloud Firestore

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.370
has been added as the
primary serving database that

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.960
stands in front of a
legacy database system.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.270
To ensure that business
processes can continue

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.270
to run on the legacy
database system,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.070
data from Cloud
Firestore is often

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.730
periodically synced
using a pipeline service

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:02.760
like Cloud Dataflow.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.920
Lastly, it's worth mentioning
that Cloud Firestore is

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.510
available in two modes.

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:16.900
Both modes utilize a shared
processing and storage layer.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:19.570
[INAUDIBLE] stored mode is
fully compatible with Cloud

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:20.330
Datastore.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:25.420
It's suitable for server
to Firestore connections.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:29.210
Native mode consists of a new
document and collections data

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.680
model.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:35.020
It also works for server
to Firestore connections.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.730
This is the mode that
you want to select

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.400
if you want to enable direct
application to Firestore

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:40.433
connections.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.320
OK, we've covered a
lot of information.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.780
Let's hear from a real customer.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.855
I would like to introduce
you to Marta Kosarchyn, who

00:08:52.855 --> 00:08:55.420
is the chief technology
officer and vice president

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:57.800
of engineering at Khan academy.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.040
Marta will be sharing
how Khan academy has

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:02.740
used Firestore to respond
to a surge of demand

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.020
of online education.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.690
MARTA KOSARCHYN: Hello,
and thank you, Minh.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:09.460
I'll start by sharing
a little about Khan

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:11.350
academy and our
mission, and then I'll

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.960
talk about how our use of
the Google Cloud Platform,

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:17.950
including Firestore, helps
us to deliver on the mission.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.960
In particular, today
I'll talk about how

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.740
Firestore, along with
App Engine for compute

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.400
enabled this to scale rapidly
in the explosion of usage

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.620
of our site beginning
with shelter in place

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.050
due to the coronavirus pandemic.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:35.200
For those of you not
familiar with Khan academy,

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.420
we're an educational nonprofit.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:40.720
And our mission is to provide
a free world-class education

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:42.860
for anyone, anywhere.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.340
Many of you will remember
the origins of Khan academy

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:49.360
as founder Sal Khan described
in his early Ted Talks, YouTube

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:52.090
videos that he made
in his walk-in closet,

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.310
motivated by his
success in remotely

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:57.580
tutoring his cousin
over the internet.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:59.740
Today, we are much more
than that collection

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.300
of YouTube videos.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.360
Today, Khan academy is the
most effective solution

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:07.040
for accelerating
measurable learning.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:10.240
We offer free interactive
instructional learning

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.210
materials and tools for
core academic subjects

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.240
from pre-school through the
first two years of college.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.310
We're a supplemental
resource for teachers

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:20.620
in the classroom
through our personalized

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.930
mastery-based
offerings, which focus

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:26.650
on fluency of foundational
skills and concepts.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:29.260
Efficacy studies show
significant impact

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:33.415
across elementary and middle
school math and in SAT prep.

00:10:33.415 --> 00:10:35.527
And I'll pause for
a few seconds to let

00:10:35.527 --> 00:10:36.610
you look at these numbers.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.200
In 2019, the Khan
Academy website

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.050
served over 94 million
registered users

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.690
in 190 countries.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:55.110
We had over 19 million
monthly active users,

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.150
which we define
as users who spend

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.640
at least 60 minutes
during the course

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.800
of the month on the site.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:06.120
We served over 240,000
teachers with content

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.470
in over 40 languages.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.760
And there were over 15
billion practice questions

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.740
completed on the site.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:18.330
In 2020, beginning in mid-March,
as schools all over the world

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.760
abruptly closed because of
the pandemic, and students,

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:25.590
parents, and teachers scrambled
to move to distance learning,

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:29.120
within just two weeks, Khan
Academy site usage grew to 2

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:32.070
1/2 times what it was at
the same time last year.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.770
And we've sustained
that level through June.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.440
We were able to respond
to this rapid growth,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.780
offering our high quality
content and classroom

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.650
experience as usual for free.

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:46.200
And in the month of April alone,
we served 30 million learners

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:47.910
on our platform.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.830
A recent national
survey of parents

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:54.150
found that Khan Academy was
the most used online resource.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.280
I'm proud that we
absorbed this rapid growth

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.320
without disrupting our users.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.470
In addition to reacting quickly
to alleviate pressure points

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:04.440
within a few days, the
Khan engineering team

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.150
had prepared in advance.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.910
And that preparation
paid dividends.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.660
We scaled readily in large part
because of our architecture

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:14.610
and our rigorous
practice of choosing

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:20.280
external services carefully,
and using them properly.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:23.010
Two fundamental components
of our architecture service

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:27.420
particularly well here,
server-less and caching.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:30.330
We use GCP, including
App Engine, Datastore,

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:34.500
and Memcached, as well as
other peripheral GCP services.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:36.030
And these three
were the backbone

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:38.310
of the server-less
and caching strategy

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.900
that's key to our scalability.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.080
As you can see in this
diagram, server-less

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.120
is a big lever for us.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.940
Using App Engine, a fully
managed environment,

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:51.570
means we can scale very easily
with virtually no effort.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.730
Even with a substantial
traffic increase,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:57.900
our site stayed up and performed
well with minimal intervention.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.510
We didn't need to worry about
load balancing ourselves

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:03.960
because server instances
were brought up as needed.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.870
Caching also plays a
key role in our scaling.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:10.260
We use Fastly CDN, which allows
us to cache all static data

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:12.570
and minimize server chips.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.630
Huge for scalability, it
also helps us optimize

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:17.560
hosting resources.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:21.360
We also extensively cache common
queries, user preferences,

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:22.920
and session data.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:26.977
And we leverage this to speed
up data fetching performance.

00:13:26.977 --> 00:13:28.810
I know you want to hear
about the third work

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:32.600
horse in our mostly
effortless scaling, Firestore.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.480
Firestore scales out storage and
access capacity automatically

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.790
in much the same way that
App Engine scales out

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:40.950
web server instances.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:44.190
We spend very little time
worrying about storage.

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:47.010
Indeed, of the major
parts of our system,

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:50.040
it was the only one we didn't
have to [INAUDIBLE] at all.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.010
App Engine, scaling,
memcache, and task queues

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.040
did require a little
tuning, although minimal.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:57.840
It's really hard
to overemphasize

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:01.800
how easily Firestore scales
without us doing anything

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:02.700
at all.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:05.700
We have no need to
worry about resharding

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.190
or ever about adding capacity.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.340
Here's a look at
our June numbers

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:15.540
to give you a sense of the scale
of our data and site traffic

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:18.600
even in the summer slow month.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.350
Note the 5 to 10x daily
scale-out of sight traffic.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.320
The auto-scaling of both
App Engine and Firestore

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:29.770
helps us to minimize costs since
we pay only for what we used.

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:32.550
I'll end by sharing a couple
of the amazing stories

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:34.020
that our grateful
users have been

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.960
sharing with us about
their experiences

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:39.120
as the pandemic started.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.000
Here's one from a teacher.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.720
I'll pause to let you read that.

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:03.220
And here's another
from a doctor working

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.780
on the pandemic front lines.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:34.850
And finally, I want to remind
you that we're a non-profit.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.740
Thanks for having
me today, Minh.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:41.930
Back to you.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:43.100
MINH NGUYEN: Thanks, Marta.

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.980
It's exciting to see Khan
Academy's broad impact

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.380
at an acute time of need.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.840
The Cloud Firestore team is
humbled to help customers

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.560
like you make that possible.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:58.390
Next, I'd like to share
some product announcements.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.940
We wrapped up 2019 with
a class of features

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:04.630
to support richer
queries on Firestore.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:06.780
This includes
collection groups, along

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:10.500
with IN and
ARRAY-CONTAINS-ANY queries.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.340
We also added support for
increment and decrement

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:14.150
operations.

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:20.640
More recently, we made it easy
for Unity and C++ developers

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.770
to adopt Firestore.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:26.250
It's also now simple to
programmatically create

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:29.320
Firestore databases
using Terraform.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:30.810
And you can create
those databases

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:34.440
in four new locations bringing
our total global footprint

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:36.570
to 19 locations
around the world.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:42.600
Moving forward, a major theme
of investment per Firestore

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.730
includes metrics and
performance optimization tools.

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.790
To support this, we have
launched usage dashboards

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:53.870
and APIs to improve
throughput of both bulk writes

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:57.020
and read operations.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:59.690
One of the more exciting
features that is coming soon

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:02.420
is a new performance profiler
for Cloud Firestore called

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:05.577
Key Visualizer.

00:17:05.577 --> 00:17:07.119
And with that, I
want to hand it over

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:12.891
to Arash, who will be digging a
bit deeper into Key Visualizer.

00:17:12.891 --> 00:17:14.099
ARASH PARSA: Thank you, Minh.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:15.089
Hi.

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:17.880
My name is Arash Parsa, and
I'm a software engineer here

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.490
at Cal Firestore.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:21.930
I'll be talking about
Firestore Key Visualizer, which

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:24.430
is an exciting new feature we
will be launching at the alpha

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:26.670
soon.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:29.060
Cloud Firestore is built
for automatically scaling

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:31.720
and high performance
out of the box.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.650
There's still, sometimes,
when we talk to our customers,

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:37.640
we hear statements such
as, I'm experiencing

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:40.430
medium latency of
hundreds of milliseconds

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.200
for my lookup operations.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.850
Is that expected?

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:48.110
Or, I'm experiencing p99 latency
of order of a few seconds

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:49.870
for my write operations.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.380
Is that normal?

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:53.370
And often, the answer is no.

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:55.670
We commonly find these
performance problems

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:58.940
to be due to the incoming
traffic violating the server's

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:01.390
best practices.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:03.960
This was an example to
explore one of the ways

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:07.920
an implementation might bite
at these best practices.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:09.720
I am a new Cloud
Firestore customer,

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.520
and I am working on
launching a new API.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:15.750
In this API, I will simply
receive documents and store

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.060
them in my Firestore database.

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:20.670
In this case, I've
implemented a function that

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:22.890
generates the
document IDs for me,

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.380
and this function generates
these document IDs

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:26.910
incrementally.

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:30.150
It's worth noting that we
also find these incremental IP

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:32.850
patterns often when
customers are importing

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.920
data from other
databases, such as MySQL,

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.100
where autoincrementing
ideas are common.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:41.040
Let's see what kind
of traffic pattern

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.530
this implementation
would result to.

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:46.660
As you can see in
this picture, this

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:50.590
will result with sequential
write pattern in my key-space.

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:54.410
Keys are the full paths
to your documents.

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:56.140
So for any given
document, the key

00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:00.670
is going to include all the
time collections in addition

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:04.740
to the document idea, which
is the last part of the key.

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.980
Firestore is a [INAUDIBLE]
sharded service,

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.950
and at any point in
time, your key-space

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:13.990
is going to map to a number
of underlining shards.

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:16.220
The undesired property
of this write pattern

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.550
is that at any point in
time, your note is focused

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:22.780
on a single underlying shard.

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:25.390
Cloud Firestore supports
sequential loads

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:28.660
at up to 500
requests per second.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:32.310
At higher levels of load,
this can result in hotspot.

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:34.030
And for example,
it can show itself

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:38.980
in your API in the form of
elevated latencies or errors.

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:41.560
Cloud Firestore's automatically
scaling capabilities

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.950
enables you to scale up
your load to millions

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.200
of requests per second.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.510
More specifically,
by following our ramp

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:52.240
up guideline, which suggests
to generally increase

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:55.930
your build by 50%
every five minutes,

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:59.230
you can scale up your
load to tens of millions

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:01.210
of requests per second.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:03.730
That's still in a
[INAUDIBLE] sharded service

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:06.220
and sequential note
pattern continues to map

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:11.610
to a single shard, and hence,
can result in hotspots.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.850
At Cloud Firestore,
we are constantly

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:16.010
working to provide you
with better features that

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:20.130
can help you diagnose these
type of performance problems.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:24.540
We are excited to launch Key
Visualizer in alpha soon.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:26.880
This tool provides
you with visibility

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.400
into the traffic
patterns of your database

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:32.010
across the key-space,
and helps you

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:36.152
self-diagnose a number of
different performance problems.

00:20:36.152 --> 00:20:37.610
Let's go back to
the customer story

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.590
that we were just reviewing.

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:42.600
So in preparation
for my API launch,

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.580
I set up load tests
that can hopefully

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:47.790
represent the traffic
that I'm going to receive

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.470
during and after my launch.

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:54.010
During these load tests,
I start to observe

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:56.830
that I'm experiencing
some poor performance

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:00.030
for my write operations.

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:01.810
I jumped to the
Key Visualizer view

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.780
in order to start to
the debugging process

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:05.530
and get an understanding
of where the root

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:09.140
cause of the problem might be.

00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:13.240
Cloud Firestore Key Visualizer
provides two dimensional heat

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:17.460
maps of your project's traffic.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:20.640
In these heat map views,
the horizontal axis

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:24.840
is time, increasing
from left to right,

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:28.440
and the vertical axis
is your key-space,

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:31.530
with the lower key values
located at the bottom,

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:36.750
and your largest key
is located at the top.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.680
Firestore Key Visualizer heat
map views are two hours wide.

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:46.270
The heating scaled
at the top left

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:50.650
provides the color coding
that is used in this heat map.

00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:54.940
In particular, I can see
that black, or dark purple,

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:58.570
indicates that the metric
value is one of the lower

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:03.160
values versus the glowing
whites, which indicates

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.380
those regions are carrying
some of the higher

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:06.470
values for this metric.

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:12.190
Firestore Key
Visualizer provides

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:17.650
a number of different metrics,
including your total QPS.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:19.780
In addition to the
QPS broken down

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:25.360
for your write, look up,
and Query operations,

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:30.070
it also includes latency metrics
for these three RPC categories.

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:36.040
Going back to the example
that we were just reviewing,

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:40.870
I start by examining the
write latencies metric.

00:22:40.870 --> 00:22:43.480
Using the provided
color coding here,

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:46.630
I can identify that there
is a sequential load

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:52.270
pattern that is accompanied
with elevated levels of latency.

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:55.480
In particular, I
can find regions

00:22:55.480 --> 00:23:01.490
where the average latency can
go as high as a few seconds.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:03.730
This is certainly not
good and indicates

00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:05.470
that there is a
serious performance

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:09.420
degradation happening.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:14.450
Next, I switched to
the write QPS metric.

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:18.290
Using this metric, I can observe
that this sequential load

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:25.500
pattern is operating at higher
than 5,000 requests per second.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:29.190
Thinking back about Cloud
Firestore best practices,

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:33.840
I recall that sequential load
patterns are well supported up

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:39.000
to 500 requests per second,
but otherwise at higher levels

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:42.240
of load, they can result
in hotspots and performance

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:45.020
problems.

00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:47.390
I can confirm with
my application code

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:50.270
that the document IDs
within this connection

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.110
are in fact being
set sequentially.

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:55.670
And this is the likely
source and root cause

00:23:55.670 --> 00:23:57.960
of this performance problem.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:01.370
In this case, I'll go ahead
and change my implementation

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:04.550
to use the Firestore
autogenerated IDs, which

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:10.790
are randomized by default, and
redeploy my code to production.

00:24:10.790 --> 00:24:13.730
Next, I can use Key
Visualizer to confirm

00:24:13.730 --> 00:24:16.190
that my traffic is
now well distributed

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:20.120
across my key-space, and that
my latencies have moved back

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:22.030
to the normal range.

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:23.190
OK, great.

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:26.630
I was able to use Key
Visualizer to efficiently debug

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:29.250
and resolve a performance issue.

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:32.120
Now, there are a number
of other use cases

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:34.670
that you can actually use Key
Visualizer for performance

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:35.720
debugging.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:37.860
For instance, in this
case as a customer,

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:40.820
I am able to quickly identify
that collection fuel is

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:43.910
the one that is having
performance issues.

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:46.230
Even though my write
operations look

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:48.750
to be pretty well
distributed in this case,

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:51.920
it seems like there is other
reason that my writes are

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:54.940
performing poorly.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:57.700
I start by examining
the documents which

00:24:57.700 --> 00:24:59.680
are being retained
in this collection

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:01.630
and identify that
these documents are

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:05.350
carrying a field that
contains the time of insertion

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:06.790
of the doc.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:11.200
Cloud Firestore indexes are
strongly consistent, enhanced

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:15.150
or updated at the time
of your document writes.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:17.850
This means that if
you're performing

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:21.230
writes with documents that
have sequential field values,

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:26.560
those can also result in
hotspots under indexes.

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:29.050
In the case that you have
documents with sequential field

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:33.220
values, you should either exempt
them from indexing in the case

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:35.680
that you don't need
them for your queries,

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:38.690
or in the case that you
need them for your queries,

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:42.620
you can partition those heavy
write patterns, for instance,

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:47.480
by applying a random prefix to
those sequential field values.

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:49.690
In the case that you
do use this technique,

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:54.480
you would need to re-aggregate
the result on the read side.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.717
OK, so we've reviewed
a couple of examples

00:25:56.717 --> 00:25:58.300
where you might
experience performance

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:01.750
problems due to violating
the best practices.

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:05.613
Please continue to review our
full list of best practices

00:26:05.613 --> 00:26:07.030
in order to make
sure you're fully

00:26:07.030 --> 00:26:10.450
utilizing all of the high
performance capabilities that

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:13.030
are built into Cloud Firestore.

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:17.680
And now, I'm going to hand it
over to Minh to take us home.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:18.940
MINH NGUYEN: Thank you, Arash.

00:26:18.940 --> 00:26:21.770
And thank you to all the
